Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.040
RETO MEIER: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.230
hosted a veritable
buffet of talks

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.000
detailing the future of Cloud.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.700
If you didn't get a chance to
catch the session Google Cloud

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.160
Storage Best Practices,
then stay tuned,

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.900
because here is the recap.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.202
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.202 --> 00:00:19.570
RETO MEIER: Google Cloud
Storage offers a great set

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.130
of features for
writing, storing,

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.290
and distributing content
for whatever application

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:23.979
you're building.

00:00:23.979 --> 00:00:26.020
COLT MCANLIS: When you're
considering performance

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.012
for your GCS use
case, most people only

00:00:28.012 --> 00:00:29.470
consider whether
they should set up

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.840
the bucket to be regional,
multiregional, nearline,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.100
or cold storage.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.260
RETO MEIER: But there's
a few more things

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.330
to consider, if you'd
like to really maximize

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:38.650
your performance.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:42.070
In general, GCS is really
strong in terms of single stream

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:42.700
throughput.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.450
However, there's typically a
fixed latency cost per request.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.730
So obviously, for
the best performance,

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.190
you should really favor
larger requests in order

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.084
to optimize your throughput.

00:00:53.084 --> 00:00:55.000
COLT MCANLIS: But this
isn't always an option.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.130
Sometimes you're just
stuck with small objects.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:59.110
In which case getting
the best performance

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:01.210
means paralyzing your requests.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:03.850
So there's overlapped
fix latency costs

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.330
and avoiding resumable requests
since the overhead of round

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.610
trips on lots of small
objects can add up

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.692
to be a significant problem.

00:01:11.692 --> 00:01:13.150
RETO MEIER: Now,
when serving data,

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.910
you generally have the choice
of regional or multiregional

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:16.810
buckets.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.300
Regional is really good
for compute scenarios,

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:22.450
because you typically want your
VMs close to your data source

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.190
to maximize throughput.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.710
And you don't want the
overhead of bookkeeping

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.260
that's needed for
multiregional redundancy.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.870
You'll see higher
throughput in this case.

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.530
Multiregional, on
the other hand,

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.190
is great for serving
content since it can achieve

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:39.480
great remote latency
due to usage of CDNs

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.830
where Google will
automatically push assets

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:44.650
to the edges of its network
based upon usage patterns

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:47.147
without you having to
do any heavy lifting.

00:01:47.147 --> 00:01:48.730
COLT MCANLIS: Now,
writing data to GCS

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.770
is a completely
different problem.

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.870
Most people when
writing to a GCS bucket

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:56.020
quickly find that they
hit a wall or ceiling,

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.110
basically a point when which
things don't get any faster.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:00.880
When we see this,
we generally find

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.100
that the developers are
writing sequential file

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.470
names to their GCS buckets.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.600
The reason this causes
a performance problem

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.990
has to do with how GCS
shards and load balances

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:13.810
your write requests
internally so that they scale.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.460
When you've got
sequential names,

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.310
the balancer can't properly
segment and move things around

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:20.551
as efficiently,
hence the bottleneck.

00:02:20.551 --> 00:02:22.300
RETO MEIER: Now, to
address this, firstly,

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.520
try to submit buckets
of work in parallel.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.470
For example, the
foo and bar folders

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.690
can be uploaded in
parallel without stomping

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.020
all over each other.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.070
Secondly, consider prepending
a hash of the filename

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:36.010
to the filename itself, which,
given the distributed nature

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.840
of your hashing
algorithm, will create

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.000
a non-linear distribution
of filenames,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.400
resulting in better
load balancing.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.830
If you'd like some more details
on these GCS performance

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:46.990
optimizations as
well as a cool demo

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:51.110
on using a Hadoop with GCS,
check out the full next video.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.390
And if you want more recaps
on great Next content,

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:57.800
make sure to check out
